"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:10,412\nBrain Gym and \u201cbrain-based\u201d products\n\n4\n00:00:13,212 --> 00:00:14,806\nRub your brain buttons.\n\n5\n00:00:14,971 --> 00:00:16,957\nCross your arms and legs.\n\n6\n00:00:17,215 --> 00:00:19,363\nDrink water to switch on your brain.\n\n7\n00:00:19,753 --> 00:00:23,938\nThese exercises have been used in schools in dozens of countries.\n\n8\n00:00:24,225 --> 00:00:25,353\nDo they help learning?\n\n9\n00:00:25,523 --> 00:00:26,925\nThis is Brain Gym,\n\n10\n00:00:27,158 --> 00:00:30,101\na commercial program from the 1980s.\n\n11\n00:00:30,812 --> 00:00:32,976\nIts short movements, like crawling,\n\n12\n00:00:33,023 --> 00:00:34,766\ntracing shapes in the air,\n\n13\n00:00:34,796 --> 00:00:37,741\nand yawning, are said to improve reading,\n\n14\n00:00:37,761 --> 00:00:41,509\nfocus, and coordination between the two halves of the brain.\n\n15\n00:00:41,679 --> 00:00:48,313\nIts explanations draw on older ideas: that repeating early movements repatterns the brain,\n\n16\n00:00:48,586 --> 00:00:50,577\nthat one hemisphere should dominate,\n\n17\n00:00:50,941 --> 00:00:54,395\nand that training perception improves academic skills.\n\n18\n00:00:54,907 --> 00:00:59,188\nEach of those ideas was tested decades ago and not supported.\n\n19\n00:00:59,358 --> 00:01:04,621\nIn 2007, Keith Hyatt reviewed the research on Brain Gym itself.\n\n20\n00:01:05,122 --> 00:01:08,004\nHe found only a few peer reviewed studies,\n\n21\n00:01:08,213 --> 00:01:10,569\nmost with serious design problems,\n\n22\n00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:14,328\nand concluded the program was not supported by research.\n\n23\n00:01:14,498 --> 00:01:21,470\nBrain Gym is one example of a larger pattern: products labeled brain based or brain friendly,\n\n24\n00:01:21,832 --> 00:01:26,470\nsold with neuroscience language but little evidence that they improve learning.\n\n25\n00:01:26,640 --> 00:01:29,201\nThe grain of truth: moving is good.\n\n26\n00:01:29,684 --> 00:01:32,299\nShort bouts of exercise can give a small,\n\n27\n00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:34,578\nbrief lift in attention and mood,\n\n28\n00:01:34,688 --> 00:01:36,527\nas we saw in Series four.\n\n29\n00:01:37,031 --> 00:01:38,554\nBut that comes from the movement,\n\n30\n00:01:38,571 --> 00:01:40,517\nnot from repatterning the brain.\n\n31\n00:01:40,687 --> 00:01:41,891\nDo this instead.\n\n32\n00:01:42,327 --> 00:01:44,748\nKeep movement breaks if your students like them,\n\n33\n00:01:44,768 --> 00:01:46,255\nand drop the brain claims.\n\n34\n00:01:46,593 --> 00:01:49,017\nBefore adopting any brain based product,\n\n35\n00:01:49,073 --> 00:01:50,908\nask for controlled studies,\n\n36\n00:01:51,101 --> 00:01:53,222\npublished by people who do not sell it.\n\n37\n00:01:53,731 --> 00:01:55,594\nOne tip to share with students: take movement breaks,\n\n38\n00:01:55,850 --> 00:01:58,751\nbut skip the brain claims.\n\n39\n00:01:58,921 --> 00:02:01,587\nIf a product needs brain language to sell it,\n\n40\n00:02:01,897 --> 00:02:03,978\nask what the evidence says without it.\n\n"